> snf [[2, 4], [6, 8]]
Smith normal form of the 2x2 matrix: diag(2, 4), rank 2
  D: [[2, 0], [0, 4]]
  U: [[-2, 1], [3, -1]]
  V: [[1, 0], [0, 1]]
  U^-1: [[1, 1], [3, 2]]
  V^-1: [[1, 0], [0, 1]]
  cokernel: Z^2 / (columns) = Z/2 + Z/4
  check: U A V = D, U U^-1 = I and V V^-1 = I (unimodular), d_i | d_(i+1): verified
> hnf [[2, 4], [6, 8]]
Hermite normal form (rows) of the 2x2 matrix: rank 2
  H: [[2, 0], [0, 4]]
  U: [[-2, 1], [3, -1]]
  check: U A = H, det U = +-1, H in echelon form with positive pivots and reduced entries above them: verified
> abgroup Z/4 + Z/6
Z/4 + Z/6 = Z/2 + Z/12
  primary decomposition: Z/2 + Z/4 + Z/3
  order: 24
  rank: 0
  exponent: 12
  torsion subgroup: Z/2 + Z/12
  cyclic: no
  free: no
  generators: (2, 3) of order 2, (1, 5) of order 12
  how: the Smith normal form of the relation matrix (U R V = D, verified)
> abiso Z/4 + Z/6, Z/2 + Z/12
Z/4 + Z/6 = Z/2 + Z/12: isomorphic, both Z/2 + Z/12
  phi: (1, 0) -> (1, 3), (0, 1) -> (1, 2)
  psi = phi^-1: (1, 0) -> (2, 3), (0, 1) -> (1, 5)
  check: phi and psi are well defined (relations go to relations), psi phi = 1 and phi psi = 1 on generators
> abiso Z/4, Z/2 + Z/2
Z/4 and Z/2 + Z/2 are not isomorphic
  invariant factors: Z/4 versus Z/2 + Z/2
  how: the invariant factors of a finitely generated abelian group determine it up to isomorphism
> permgroup S4
S4: a permutation group of degree 4, order 24
  generators: (1 2 3 4), (1 2)
  base: 1, 2, 3; basic orbit lengths 4 3 2 (the order is their product); 4 strong generators
  orbits: {1, 2, 3, 4} (transitive)
  abelian: no
  derived series (orders): 24 > 12 > 4 > 1
  solvable: yes
  lower central series (orders): 24 > 12
  nilpotent: no
  centre: order 1
  how: Schreier-Sims (deterministic): every Schreier generator of every level sifts to the identity
  check: every generator sifts through the stabilizer chain and its word rebuilds it: verified
> permelem S3, (1 2 3)
(1 2 3): order 3; in S3
  word: p = u2(2) u1(2) (u_i(b): the transversal element of level i taking the base point to b)
  check: the product of the transversal elements is p
> cayley [[0, 1], [1, 0]]
a group of order 2 (identity 0)
  checked: closure, identity, inverses, associativity (all 8 triples)
  abelian: yes
  element orders: 1 2
  solvable: yes
  nilpotent: yes
  how: the regular representation (x -> x a) as a permutation group, order 2
> groebner x^2+y^2-1, x^3-y
  input: f1 = x^2 + y^2 - 1, f2 = x^3 - y
reduced Groebner basis (grevlex, x > y, over Q): 3 elements
  g1: y^4 - x*y - 2*y^2 + 1
  g2: x*y^2 - x + y
  g3: x^2 + y^2 - 1
  g1 = (-x^2 + y^2 - 1)*f1 + (x)*f2
  g2 = (x)*f1 + (-1)*f2
  g3 = (1)*f1
  pairs: 6; reduced: 3; skipped by the criteria: 3
  how: Buchberger (normal strategy, product and chain criteria), every element carrying its cofactors
  check: each g_i = sum c_ij f_j multiplied out; each f_j reduces to 0; every S-pair of the basis reduces to 0: verified
> idealmember x in x^2, x^3
x is not in the ideal (grevlex, x, over Q)
  normal form: x (nonzero)
  how: the remainder on division by a reduced Groebner basis (1 element, verified) is unique, and zero exactly for members
> bigint 10!
10! = 3628800
  bits: 22
  how: exact arbitrary-precision integers (32-bit limbs; Knuth division)
